<GuidePage title='The Goals - App Router Guide' root='..'>
  <div class='kicker'>Chapter 2</div>
  <h1>The Goals</h1>
  <p class='lede'>
    What you are optimizing, on every page, every time. Patterns come later; this chapter is only
    about ends, so that when techniques conflict you know which one wins.
  </p>

  <h2 id='thesis'>The thesis</h2>
  <p>
    Every page you build divides into two kinds of content: what can be
    <strong>known</strong> the moment the first render happens, and what cannot. Your job (the actual
    job, underneath every framework feature and pattern in this guide) is to:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Separate</strong> the known from the unknown, deliberately, on every page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Maximize the known</strong>: make the first render carry as much finished content as
      possible.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Contain the unknown</strong>: the parts you must wait for may not disturb anything
      around them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Decouple the parts</strong>, so each region's requirements can change without rewiring
      its neighbors. Decoupling buys more than safe change: a component that asks for its own data
      instead of receiving it through props is <em>portable</em>, so it can be dropped onto any page
      without a chain of wiring following it (<a href='15-minimize-the-wires.html'>§15</a>), and it
      lets the interactive parts that need hydration be pushed down into small leaves instead of
      dragging whole regions across the client boundary (<a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html'
        >§5</a
      >).
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Server-side rendering is what makes this framing powerful: in a browser-only app, almost
    <strong>nothing is knowable at first render</strong>: the client starts blank and earns every
    pixel through requests. On the server, the page's structure, its static content, and any data
    the server can fetch are all knowable
    <em>before the first byte reaches the browser</em>. The App Router
    <strong>exists to make that surface as large as possible</strong>. Treat it like an SPA and you
    shrink the known back down to a spinner.
  </p>

