  </Diagram>

  <p>
    The three meta-goals below organize the rest of this document. Each sub-goal is an
    <em>end</em>, tagged by scope; the chapters of Parts A–C cover the techniques (the means) for
    achieving them.
  </p>

  <div class='goal-card goal-a'>
    <div class='goal-head'>
      <span class='goal-letter'>A</span>
      <h3 id='goal-a'>Maximize the Known</h3>
    </div>
    <p class='goal-tag'>
      Make the first render carry as much finished content as possible. Elaborated in
      <a href='04-ssr.html'>§4</a>–<a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html'>§9</a>.
    </p>
    <ul class='goal-list'>
      <li>
        <span class='g-text'
          >Maximize above-the-fold content delivered as real HTML on the first response, not shells
          that earn their content later.</span
        ><span class='g-chips'><Chip k='next' /></span><span class='g-ref'
          ><a href='04-ssr.html'>§4</a></span
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class='g-text'
          >Push interactivity down as far as humanly possible: client boundaries at the leaves (a
          button, a filter, an input), never at pages or layouts.</span
        ><span class='g-chips'><Chip k='next' /></span><span class='g-ref'
          ><a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html'>§5</a></span
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class='g-text'
          >Minimize the JavaScript required for first render: anything not needed at first paint
          loads behind a dynamic boundary.</span
        ><span class='g-chips'><Chip k='react' /></span><span class='g-ref'
          ><a href='07-first-render-js.html'>§7</a></span
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class='g-text'
          >Prefer state the server can resolve (the URL, route params, search params, <a
            href='https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/functions/cookies'
            target='_blank'
            rel='noopener'>cookies</a
          >) over client state the server can't see.</span
        ><span class='g-chips'><Chip k='next' /></span><span class='g-ref'
          ><a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html'>§9</a></span
        >
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class='goal-card goal-b'>
    <div class='goal-head'>
      <span class='goal-letter'>B</span>
      <h3 id='goal-b'>Contain the Unknown</h3>
    </div>
    <p class='goal-tag'>
      The parts you must wait for may not disturb anything around them. Elaborated in
      <a href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a>–<a href='14-blocking-requests.html'
        >§14</a
      >.
    </p>
    <ul class='goal-list'>
      <li>
        <span class='g-text'
          >Minimize content shifting. Containers are known: they render immediately and reserve
          accurate space for both the fallback and the real content.</span
        ><span class='g-chips'><Chip k='react' /></span><span class='g-ref'
          ><a href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a></span
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class='g-text'
          >Loading and error states belong to boundaries, not components: a component never shows
          its own spinner.</span
        ><span class='g-chips'><Chip k='react' /></span><span class='g-ref'
          ><a href='11-suspense-streaming.html'>§11</a></span
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class='g-text'
          >Minimize what sits behind any single blocking request, on the server and the client
          alike.</span
        ><span class='g-chips'><Chip k='react' /></span><span class='g-ref'
          ><a href='14-blocking-requests.html'>§14</a></span
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class='g-text'
          >Isolate data dependencies, so a change in what a region needs affects only the region it
          determines, never the surrounding content.</span
        ><span class='g-chips'><Chip k='react' /></span><span class='g-ref'
          ><a href='11-suspense-streaming.html'>§11</a>–<a href='14-blocking-requests.html'>§14</a
          ></span
        >
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class='goal-card goal-c'>
    <div class='goal-head'>
      <span class='goal-letter'>C</span>
      <h3 id='goal-c'>Decouple the Parts</h3>
    </div>
    <p class='goal-tag'>
      Minimize the wires that connect parts of the UI together. Elaborated in
      <a href='15-minimize-the-wires.html'>§15</a>–<a href='17-component-breakdown.html'>§17</a>.
    </p>
    <ul class='goal-list'>
      <li>
        <span class='g-text'
          >Minimize data exchange between components and modules: never pass a child anything the
          parent doesn't itself need.</span
        ><span class='g-chips'><Chip k='react' /></span><span class='g-ref'
          ><a href='15-minimize-the-wires.html'>§15</a></span
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class='g-text'
          >Prefer repetition backed by dedupe over shared plumbing: it is better for two components
          to ask the same question than to be wired together; deduped fetches and cached functions
          make the repetition free.</span
        ><span class='g-chips'><Chip k='next' /></span><span class='g-ref'
          ><a href='16-dedupe-caching.html'>§16</a></span
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class='g-text'
          >Communicate through the URL and the cache, not through callbacks up to parents.</span
        ><span class='g-chips'><Chip k='react' /></span><span class='g-ref'
          ><a href='15-minimize-the-wires.html'>§15</a>,
          <a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html'>§9</a></span
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class='g-text'
          >Break components down by UI <em>and</em> responsibility - small, portable, one component per
          file.</span
        ><span class='g-chips'><Chip k='react' /></span><span class='g-ref'
          ><a href='17-component-breakdown.html'>§17</a></span
        >
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id='tension'>When goals collide</h2>
  <p>
    These goals occasionally pull against each other, and it's worth knowing the tiebreakers in
    advance. Containing the unknown (B) can tempt you to over-fragment a page into a dozen <a
      href='https://react.dev/reference/react/Suspense'
      target='_blank'
      rel='noopener'>suspense boundaries</a
    >, but decoupling (C) is about <em>dependencies</em>, not confetti; a boundary should trace a
    real data dependency, not a visual whim. Maximizing the known (A) can tempt you to block the
    whole page on a fetch so everything arrives "known", but that violates B: knowledge that arrives
    late and all-at-once is worse than structure that arrives instantly with the slow parts
    contained. When in doubt:
    <strong>structure first, then contain, then optimize what's inside</strong>.
  </p>

  <Callout kind='info' title='Why no importance chips in this chapter'>
    <p>
      Goals are ends, not rules: there's nothing here to have an exception to. The importance chips
      (<Chip k='critical' />
      <Chip k='very' />
      <Chip k='imp' />) appear from
      <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html'>§5</a> onward, where concrete practices start.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</GuidePage>
